---
name: object-apply
description: "a companion-style `object O { def apply(…) }` is callable as O(…)"
backends: [int, jvm, v2]
---

# `object O { def apply(x) }` is callable as `O(x)`

Plain Scala: an `object` with an `apply` member is applied by writing `O(x)`. The reference lane
supported it and the DEFAULT lane did not — `ssc: unbound global: O`, loud and immediate rather than
a wrong answer, which is why it survived as a low-priority entry
(`v2-object-apply-unbound`).

**`O.apply(7)` already worked, and that is the whole diagnosis.** The member IS emitted, as
`O_apply`; what was missing is that an APPLIED bare `O` never became that call. `O` is uppercase, so
it is lexed as an uppercase-name token and reaches the CONSTRUCTOR path — never the call path — and
the callee stayed `(global O)`, which no definition provides. A first attempt in `calleeOf` changed
nothing, which is how the ctor path was found.

Both spellings are here on purpose: the qualified one is the control that already passed, so a later
change cannot fix one and break the other.

A SECOND object with its own `apply` is deliberately NOT here. It breaks the INT lane — its JIT
emits one Java class per method name and two `apply(long, long)` members collide
(`method apply(long,long) is already defined`), filed as
`int-jit-two-object-applies-collide`. Including it would pin an unrelated lane's defect in a case
about this one.

`js` is deliberately absent — it fails with `not callable`, a different cause on a lane with its own
object emission, filed separately rather than folded in. **The `SSC_FRONT=legacy` fallback is also
still unfixed**; the entry carries the measurement.

```scalascript
object O:
  def apply(x: Int): String = "user-apply:" + x.toString

case class C(n: Int)

def main() =
  println(O(7))
  println(O.apply(7))
  // a case class must STILL construct — objReg holds objects only, and this row is what
  // keeps the new branch from swallowing ordinary constructor application
  println(C(5).n)
```
